The **SOLID** principles are a set of five design principles in object-oriented programming (OOP) that help developers create maintainable, scalable, and flexible software. They were introduced by **Robert C. Martin** (Uncle Bob) and are widely used in software engineering. Here’s a breakdown of each principle:

---

### **1. Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)**
**Definition:** A class should have only **one reason to change**, meaning it should have only **one job** or responsibility.

**Why?**
- Avoids classes becoming too complex or bloated.
- Makes code easier to maintain and test.

**Example:**
❌ **Bad:**
```java
class User {
    void saveUserToDatabase(User user) { ... }
    void sendEmailToUser(User user) { ... }
}
```
✅ **Good:**
```java
class User {
    // Only handles user data
}

class UserRepository {
    void saveUserToDatabase(User user) { ... }
}

class EmailService {
    void sendEmailToUser(User user) { ... }
}
```

---

### **2. Open/Closed Principle (OCP)**
**Definition:** Software entities (classes, modules, functions) should be **open for extension** but **closed for modification**.

**Why?**
- Prevents breaking existing code when adding new features.
- Encourages using **abstraction** (interfaces, abstract classes).

**Example:**
❌ **Bad (modifying existing code):**
```java
class AreaCalculator {
    double calculateArea(Object shape) {
        if (shape instanceof Circle) {
            return ((Circle) shape).radius * ((Circle) shape).radius * Math.PI;
        } else if (shape instanceof Square) {
            return ((Square) shape).side * ((Square) shape).side;
        }
        // Adding a new shape requires modifying this method!
    }
}
```
✅ **Good (extending via abstraction):**
```java
interface Shape {
    double area();
}

class Circle implements Shape {
    double radius;
    public double area() { return radius * radius * Math.PI; }
}

class Square implements Shape {
    double side;
    public double area() { return side * side; }
}

class AreaCalculator {
    double calculateArea(Shape shape) {
        return shape.area(); // No modification needed for new shapes!
    }
}
```

---

### **3. Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)**
**Definition:** Subtypes must be **substitutable** for their base types without altering the correctness of the program.

**Why?**
- Ensures inheritance is used correctly.
- Prevents unexpected behavior when replacing a parent class with a child class.

**Example:**
❌ **Bad (violates LSP):**
```java
class Bird {
    void fly() { System.out.println("Flying"); }
}

class Ostrich extends Bird {
    @Override
    void fly() { throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Ostriches can't fly!"); }
}
```
✅ **Good (separate interfaces):**
```java
interface Flyable {
    void fly();
}

interface Walkable {
    void walk();
}

class Bird implements Walkable { ... }
class Eagle implements Flyable, Walkable { ... }
class Ostrich implements Walkable { ... }
```

---

### **4. Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)**
**Definition:** Clients should not be forced to depend on interfaces they do not use. **Split large interfaces into smaller, specific ones.**

**Why?**
- Avoids "fat" interfaces that force unnecessary implementations.
- Improves modularity and reduces side effects.

**Example:**
❌ **Bad (one big interface):**
```java
interface Worker {
    void work();
    void eat();
}

class HumanWorker implements Worker { ... }
class RobotWorker implements Worker {
    void eat() { throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Robots don't eat!"); }
}
```
✅ **Good (separate interfaces):**
```java
interface Workable {
    void work();
}

interface Eatable {
    void eat();
}

class HumanWorker implements Workable, Eatable { ... }
class RobotWorker implements Workable { ... }
```

---

### **5. Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)**
**Definition:**
1. **High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules. Both should depend on abstractions.**
2. **Abstractions should not depend on details. Details should depend on abstractions.**

**Why?**
- Reduces tight coupling between components.
- Makes code more reusable and testable.

**Example:**
❌ **Bad (tight coupling):**
```java
class LightBulb {
    void turnOn() { System.out.println("LightBulb: On"); }
    void turnOff() { System.out.println("LightBulb: Off"); }
}

class Switch {
    private LightBulb bulb;
    void operate() {
        bulb.turnOn(); // Direct dependency on LightBulb
    }
}
```
✅ **Good (depends on abstraction):**
```java
interface Switchable {
    void turnOn();
    void turnOff();
}

class LightBulb implements Switchable { ... }

class Fan implements Switchable { ... }

class Switch {
    private Switchable device;
    void operate() {
        device.turnOn(); // Works with any Switchable device
    }
}
```

---

### **Summary Table**
| Principle | Acronym | Key Idea | Benefit |
|-----------|---------|----------|---------|
| **Single Responsibility** | SRP | One class, one job | Easier maintenance |
| **Open/Closed** | OCP | Extend without modifying | Flexible & scalable |
| **Liskov Substitution** | LSP | Subtypes must be substitutable | Correct inheritance |
| **Interface Segregation** | ISP | Small, specific interfaces | Avoids bloat |
| **Dependency Inversion** | DIP | Depend on abstractions | Loose coupling |

---

### **Why Follow SOLID?**
✅ **Maintainability** – Easier to update and debug.
✅ **Scalability** – New features can be added without breaking existing code.
✅ **Reusability** – Components can be reused across projects.
✅ **Testability** – Easier to write unit tests.
✅ **Understandability** – Code is modular and self-documenting.

